----------------------------------------------------------------------
Source data attributions
----------------------------------------------------------------------

This dataset incorporates and is derived from the following public sources.
All consumers must preserve these attributions per ODbL-1.0 §4.3.

  - OpenStreetMap (https://www.openstreetmap.org)
    © OpenStreetMap contributors, licensed ODbL-1.0.
    Used as the topological scaffold (footways, crossings, road centerlines).

  - NYC DOT Pedestrian Ramp Locations (dataset id: ufzp-rrqu)
    https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Transportation/Pedestrian-Ramp-Locations/ufzp-rrqu
    NYC Open Data terms of use, no licence attached. Used for curb-ramp
    locations, measured slopes and warning-surface status.

  - NYC Planimetric Database: Sidewalks (dataset id: 52n9-sdep)
    https://data.cityofnewyork.us/City-Government/NYC-Planimetric-Database-Sidewalk/52n9-sdep
    NYC Open Data terms of use, no licence attached. Used for sidewalk
    widths and as the source of the gap-fill centrelines shipped beside
    the graph.

  - Borough boundaries: OpenStreetMap, fetched through Nominatim. The
    pipeline first tries NYC Borough Boundaries (dataset id: 7t3b-ywvw),
    which NYC Open Data has withdrawn, so the fallback is what ships.
    Used as region polygons and to cut the per-borough graphs from the
    city graph.

  - NYC 2017 Topobathymetric LiDAR bare-earth DTM
    Served by the NY State GIS Program Office ArcGIS ImageServer
    (https://elevation.its.ny.gov/, NYC_TopoBathymetric_2017_1_meter).
    Public data, no licence attached. Fetched as 2 m tiles to derive node
    elevations and edge inclines.

  - LiDAR point clouds: the 2017 NYC topobathymetric survey (NYC OTI) and
    the 2014 USGS survey of New York City, served as Entwine Point Tiles on
    NOAA's Digital Coast archive
    (https://noaa-nos-coastal-lidar-pds.s3.amazonaws.com/entwine/geoid18/9306
    and .../4920). Published with no licence attached; treated as public
    domain. Used for deck heights on bridges and elevated ways.

  - OpenSidewalks Schema v0.3
    https://sidewalks.washington.edu/opensidewalks/0.3/schema.json
    Developed by the Taskar Center for Accessible Technology, University of
    Washington. The schema specifies the OSW feature types and properties;
    this dataset is checked against it with python-osw-validation 0.5.0.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
AI-assisted authoring disclosure
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Portions of the pipeline code, conversion scripts, and documentation in this
repository were drafted with the assistance of large language models
(Anthropic Claude). All AI-suggested changes were reviewed, edited, and
accepted by a human author who takes responsibility for the resulting code,
the methodology, and the dataset. AI tools were not used to generate or
modify the source data itself.

Language-model agents were also used as raters. In the check of the ramp to
crossing rule and in the router comparison's disagreement sheets, each sheet
of aerial imagery was rated by language-model agents following a written
protocol, and a sample was rated again by another agent for agreement. Every
agreement statistic (kappa) quoted in this repository is agreement between
such agents. No person rated those sheets, and nothing was checked on the
ground. The protocols and ratings are in evaluation/.
